WEBVTT

00:00:00.673 --> 00:00:01.698
Pronouns.

00:00:01.698 --> 00:00:04.969
Trace the pronoun back to its noun, then make them match.

00:00:06.511 --> 00:00:08.136
Here's how to spot one.

00:00:08.136 --> 00:00:12.336
The choices are pronouns, like it, its, they, or their.

00:00:12.336 --> 00:00:15.843
And the noun they refer to is often far back in the sentence.

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.477
It's testing whether the pronoun matches the noun it replaces.

00:00:21.511 --> 00:00:22.786
Here's the method.

00:00:22.786 --> 00:00:24.586
Find the noun it replaces.

00:00:24.586 --> 00:00:27.156
Trace it back, even past commas.

00:00:27.156 --> 00:00:29.691
Count it: one, or more than one?

00:00:29.691 --> 00:00:31.701
Organizations count as one.

00:00:31.701 --> 00:00:32.976
Pick the match.

00:00:32.976 --> 00:00:34.601
One takes it and its.

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:36.676
More than one takes they and their.

00:00:36.676 --> 00:00:40.063
Then check the form: its versus it's, who versus whom.

00:00:41.139 --> 00:00:44.999
Rule one: companies, teams, and museums are singular.

00:00:44.999 --> 00:00:47.574
The museum changed their hours is wrong.

00:00:47.574 --> 00:00:49.549
The museum changed its hours.

00:00:50.606 --> 00:00:54.781
Rule two: its, with no apostrophe, means belonging to it.

00:00:54.781 --> 00:00:57.606
It's, with an apostrophe, means it is.

00:00:57.606 --> 00:01:00.561
So the town and its festival has no apostrophe.

00:01:01.639 --> 00:01:06.609
Rule three: after words like to, for, and with, use me, him, or them.

00:01:06.609 --> 00:01:08.119
Not to Priya and I.

00:01:08.119 --> 00:01:09.444
To Priya and me.

00:01:09.444 --> 00:01:11.505
Test it by dropping Priya: to me.

00:01:12.548 --> 00:01:13.873
Let's try one.

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:16.473
Skip the extra part between the commas.

00:01:16.473 --> 00:01:17.748
Who's celebrating?

00:01:17.748 --> 00:01:18.893
The orchestra.

00:01:18.893 --> 00:01:20.678
One group, so singular.

00:01:20.678 --> 00:01:21.928
Their is plural.

00:01:21.928 --> 00:01:24.453
It's, with an apostrophe, means it is.

00:01:24.453 --> 00:01:25.953
They're means they are.

00:01:25.953 --> 00:01:29.363
Its, with no apostrophe, means belonging to the orchestra.

00:01:29.363 --> 00:01:30.747
The answer is B.

00:01:31.806 --> 00:01:34.006
Watch out for the plural decoy.

00:01:34.006 --> 00:01:37.056
Teachers sits right there, pulling you toward their.

00:01:37.056 --> 00:01:39.141
But the teachers aren't celebrating.

00:01:39.141 --> 00:01:40.490
The orchestra is.

00:01:41.544 --> 00:01:43.414
Here's the lesson on one page.

00:01:43.414 --> 00:01:44.474
How to spot it.

00:01:44.474 --> 00:01:45.824
The four steps.

00:01:45.824 --> 00:01:46.929
The rules.

00:01:46.929 --> 00:01:47.954
And the trap.

00:01:47.954 --> 00:01:50.713
Pause the video now, and copy it into your notes.
